To create an animation that illustrates the concept of the slope of the line y = 2x - 1, we will use Manim, a mathematical animation engine. The goal is to visually represent the slope of the line using a "Rise/Run" or "stairs" animation, which intuitively shows how for every one unit moved to the right (run), the line moves up by two units (rise), thus demonstrating a slope of 2.

## Step 1: Setting Up the Scene
First, we set up a Manim scene that includes a coordinate system (Axes) where our line y = 2x - 1 will be drawn.

## Step 2: Drawing the Line
We then draw the line y = 2x - 1 on these axes. This line represents our function and will be the central element of our animation.

## Step 3: Creating the Rise/Run Animation
To illustrate the slope, we create a "Rise/Run" animation. This involves:
- **Run**: Moving one unit to the right on the x-axis.
- **Rise**: Moving two units up on the y-axis, corresponding to the slope of 2.

This can be visualized by drawing a right-angled triangle where the base (run) is 1 unit and the height (rise) is 2 units, effectively demonstrating the slope of the line.

## Step 4: Animating the Slope
We animate the drawing of this triangle or the "rise over run" concept along the line at different points to emphasize that the slope is constant everywhere. This can be achieved by using Manim's animation functions to draw lines or move dots along the path of the line, illustrating the rise and run.

## Step 5: Adding Text and Labels
Finally, we add text and labels to the animation to explain what is being shown. This includes labeling the axes, the line with its equation (y = 2x - 1), and possibly adding text to highlight the slope (m = 2) and how it relates to the rise and run.

